// Blowfish, as described by Bruce Schneier:
// https://www.schneier.com/academic/blowfish/
//
// A 16-round Feistel network keyed by a deliberately expensive key schedule:
// the subkeys start as the digits of pi and are then overwritten by encrypting
// the subkeys themselves. That is what bcrypt leans on — it makes the schedule
// repeat, salted, so the cost of a guess becomes the cost of a key setup rather
// than the cost of a block.
//
// Deprecated as a cipher: 64-bit blocks are too small for a mode of operation
// to be safe in general. It is here because bcrypt is defined on top of it, and
// because the key schedule is the interesting part.
import {
  "moonbitstack/mooncrypt/spec",
}

import {
  "moonbitstack/moonbase/base16",
} for "test"
